    "knowledge_point": "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions",
    "problem": "Express $\\sqrt{3}{cos}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}-\\sqrt{3}{sin}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio.",
    "worked_solution": "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}({cos}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={35}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {35}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}({cos}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘})=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
